Reload the workbench when the browser restores it from the back/forward cache.

A bfcache restore hands back the page exactly as it was - DOM, scroll, focus -
but every socket it held is gone, so Composery reappears looking alive on top of
a dead connection and the reader has to work out that a reload is the way out.
The HTML is already Cache-Control: no-store (asset-cache.diff), which used to be
enough to keep a page out of the bfcache; Chrome on Android now caches no-store
pages anyway, so the page has to say so itself.

Costs nothing when it never happens: `persisted` is set only on a bfcache
restore, so an ordinary load registers the listener and never calls it.

diff --git a/lib/vscode/src/vs/workbench/browser/client.ts b/lib/vscode/src/vs/workbench/browser/client.ts
--- a/lib/vscode/src/vs/workbench/browser/client.ts
+++ b/lib/vscode/src/vs/workbench/browser/client.ts
@@ -117,12 +117,26 @@
 		});
 
 		this.addShowQrCommand();
+		this.reloadOnBackForwardRestore();
 
 		if (this.productService.serviceWorker) {
 			await this.registerServiceWorker(this.productService.serviceWorker);
 		}
 	}
 
+	// Composery: a page restored from the back/forward cache keeps its DOM but not
+	// its sockets, so the workbench comes back looking alive over a connection that
+	// died while it was away - the state VS Code can only offer to reload out of.
+	// `persisted` is true for that restore and nothing else, so an ordinary load
+	// never reaches the reload.
+	private reloadOnBackForwardRestore(): void {
+		window.addEventListener('pageshow', event => {
+			if (event.persisted) {
+				window.location.reload();
+			}
+		});
+	}
+
